# Mr. Wizard API Design Rules

## Preferred Nomenclature Consistency
    - WaveShape (not Waveform)
    - When referencing rate, clarify rate type:
      - Modulation Rate? LFO Frequency? Etc.
      - Unitize all user-facing phase parameters in degrees.  All internal phase calculations should be performed in radians.

- New development should never break potential legacy code.  When so, the designer should be alerted before moving forward.
  
- Use JUCE native methods, constants and practices where applicable. 

- All modules should have individual setters/getters to update each parameter individually, as well as a single updateParameters method to pass all arguments in one go, with any one of the arguments being optional.

- Maintain modularity and DRY principles.  When parameters are assigned internal to a class, they should use the designated internal setters/getters rather than additional longform code, when possible.

- Variables and intermediate values should be heavily clarified, showing details and units.  Include units in method names when applicable.

- When tasked with developing a parent class, do NOT modify external/child classes, or any other files, to achieve the needs of the class you're developing.

- Due to the nature of my workflow, the software written in Cursor is transferred to and then built in Visual Studio.  Because of this, you cannot actually run the code you build.  You do not need to spend effort attempting to run the code.

- See design rule "config-struct" for information on how to arrange relevant parameters and their accesibility.

- In general, conform new development or relevant updates to these design rules - but do not go out of your way to tear up old code to conform it.

- Refer to the associated MD Documentation files when implementing development, but refrain from spending time updating it until asked.

# Formatting
- When adding new content, consider method positioning in order to keep relevant methods sectioned together.  DSP should remain together; setters/getters remain together, helper/utility functions remain together, etc.  For existing code, do not spend effort on moving code around if it is not the subject of discussion.  Include copious comments, explanations, formatting and white space. Examples below:

    // ============================================================================
    // CONSTRUCTOR & DESTRUCTOR
    // ============================================================================
    ~code...

    // ============================================================================
    // INDIVIDUAL PARAMETER SETTERS
    // ============================================================================
    ~code...

    // ============================================================================
    // AUDIO PROCESSING
    // ============================================================================
    ~code...